## Last text from previous session

gly mashed two different things together.

So your instinct is exactly the design principle, and it's also the bug diagnosis: **the reader-side coherency currently leans on that lossy disk *ring* (the gen-bump hint) to learn "a peer changed this directory." That's the unreliable part.** The reliable disk channel is the CAW **slot** itself, which a node already reads on every lock acquire.

That's precisely why the right fix — Gemini's and GFS2's LVB pattern — is to put a **monotonic version epoch inside the CAW slot**: the writer bumps it at release (after its durability flush), and a reader compares it during the acquire it's already doing. Reliable, on-disk, zero network, and it refreshes only when something actually changed — no per-op extra I/O like the (timing-regressing) approach I just tried.

One correction to my own last hour of work: the per-op `caw_held()` disk-read guard I built (`P108`) is the *wrong shape* — it adds a disk read to every dir op and re-introduced the timing wall (unlink's delete phase went slow). The epoch-in-slot approach gets the same correctness without that cost.

Note: I have a `cache_coherency` run finishing in the background (`/tmp/cc_p108.log`) from the P108 build — I'll read its result to confirm whether the lock-truth guard at least fixes the *correctness* failure before I rebuild around the epoch. 

Want me to proceed with ripping out the per-op `P108` guard and implementing the slot version-epoch (LVB) as the real fix?
